INT. COUNTRY HOUSE - GREAT HALL - 1923 - EVENING

A cold stone hall hung with antlers. A fire gutters in the vast hearth. At a long oak table sit four guests over the remains of a late supper.

LADY WINIFRED PEMBROKE-ADEYEMI (60s, pearls, ebony cane) presides at the head. At her right sits her nephew, REGINALD "REGGIE" ASHFORD-LU (30s, rumpled dinner jacket, flushed). Opposite him, MISS CORDELIA SANTANGELO (30s, severe black dress, a secretary's notebook), keeps her eyes on the fire. And the family physician, DR. HORACE BLACKWOOD-ESSIEN (50s, silver whiskers), swirls his brandy.

                    WINIFRED
          More claret, Horace?

                    HORACE
          Thank you, Winifred.

                    REGGIE
          Auntie, the telephone is ringing again.

                    WINIFRED
          Let it.

                    REGGIE
          It has rung six times.

                    WINIFRED
          Then it will ring a seventh.

                    CORDELIA
          (without looking up)
          I shall answer it, Lady Winifred.

She rises, crosses the flagstones, and lifts the heavy receiver from its cradle on the wall.

                    CORDELIA (CONT'D)
          Pembroke Hall.

                    CONSTABLE (V.O.)
          (tinny, through the earpiece)
          Miss Santangelo? Constable Haddock, calling from the village. I'm afraid there's been a death.

Cordelia glances at the table. The others have stopped talking. Reggie sets down his glass.

                    CORDELIA
          Whose?

                    CONSTABLE (V.O.)
          The gamekeeper, miss. Found in the lower meadow an hour ago. I thought her ladyship ought to know.

                    CORDELIA
          Thank you, Constable.

She replaces the receiver and turns back to the table.

                    WINIFRED
          Well?

                    CORDELIA
          It was Mr. Tolliver, my lady. He is dead.

Winifred lowers her cane. Horace puts down his brandy. Reggie stares at the fire. The wind howls once in the chimney.

At the far end of the hall, the great oak door creaks open. A drenched figure stands on the threshold: CONSTABLE HADDOCK (40s, rain cape, helmet in hand).

                    HADDOCK
          Beg pardon, my lady. I rode over directly. There are matters I need to ask.

                    WINIFRED
          Then come in out of the rain, Constable, and sit.

He hangs his cape on an antler and takes the empty chair at the foot of the table. The fire cracks, and no one meets anyone's eyes.
